    '_**The outgoing controller left a handover of fourteen pages organised by file:** the bank files, the supplier files, the tax file, the payroll file, the audit file._',
    '_**Every page said where something was kept** and not one said what to do with it, and the successor found the files in a week and the judgements in nine months._',
    '_**The second handover was organised by decision,** which is a different document made of the same material._',
    '_**"Decisions you will take in your first month, in the order they will arrive."** The payment run every Thursday. The month-end close on the fifth working day. The provision on the Hamdan claim at the quarter._**_',
    '_**For each one: what is decided, who decides it, what it rests on, and the last three times it was taken and why.**_**_',
    '_**That last clause is the whole value.** "The last three times" is a record of judgement, and a successor who reads three worked examples can take the fourth._',
    '_**The files then appear only as the places where evidence lives,** in two lines at the end, because finding a file is a week’s work and knowing what to do is nine months’._',
    '_**Decisions are listed in the order they arrive,** not in order of importance: the first Thursday matters more than the annual audit, which is six months away._',
    '_**And every decision names the person who will know if it goes wrong,** which is not always the person who takes it._',
